            { number: 2, prompt: "Since the April 2013 changes, notifiable work under Part P in England is limited to:", options: { A: "Any electrical work in a kitchen", B: "Replacing a single accessory", C: "Installing a new circuit, replacing a consumer unit, or any addition / alteration in a special location (e.g. a room containing a bath or shower)", D: "Only the installation of EV charging equipment" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Three categories remain notifiable in England: a new circuit, a consumer unit replacement (or main switch replacement), and any addition/alteration in a special location. Kitchens and outdoor work were removed from the notifiable list in 2013, though the work still has to comply." },
            { number: 3, prompt: "For Part P purposes, a \"special location\" in a dwelling is most commonly a:", options: { A: "A bedroom containing an en-suite shower room directly adjoining it through an internal doorway", B: "Any kitchen, since kitchens were treated as a special location prior to the April 2013 amendment", C: "Room containing a bath or shower (zones 0, 1 or 2)", D: "A hallway or stairwell that gives access to a room containing a bath or shower in the same dwelling" }, answer: "C", explanation: "The Part P definition of a special location post-2013 is essentially \"a room containing a bath or shower\" within zones 0, 1 or 2 as defined by BS 7671 Section 701. Swimming pools and saunas are also special locations under BS 7671 but are far less common in domestic work." },
            { number: 4, prompt: "Carrying out notifiable Part P work without using one of the approved compliance routes is:", options: { A: "A civil matter only — the customer can sue but no offence is committed", B: "A criminal offence under the Building Act, with the local authority empowered to require correction or removal", C: "Permitted provided BS 7671 is followed", D: "Permitted provided the customer is told" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Failing to notify is an offence under the Building Act 1984, prosecutable for up to 2 years after completion. The local authority can serve an enforcement notice requiring the work to be altered or removed; in practice the bigger problem for most owners is that they cannot prove compliance when they sell." },
            { number: 5, prompt: "An electrician adds a single fused spur to an existing kitchen ring final circuit. Under Part P this work is:", options: { A: "Notifiable because it is in a kitchen", B: "Non-notifiable, but still subject to BS 7671 and certified by Minor Works", C: "Non-notifiable and exempt from BS 7671", D: "Notifiable because the kitchen contains a sink" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Kitchens were removed from Part P's notifiable list in April 2013. An addition to an existing circuit outside a special location is non-notifiable, but still has to comply with BS 7671 and should be certified on a Minor Electrical Installation Works Certificate." },
            { number: 6, prompt: "Which of the following Part P jobs is NOT notifiable in England?", options: { A: "Replacing a domestic consumer unit", B: "Installing a new lighting circuit in a loft conversion", C: "Adding an additional socket to a hallway ring final circuit", D: "Installing a new shower circuit feeding a bathroom" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Adding a socket to an existing circuit outside a special location is non-notifiable. Consumer unit replacement, new circuits, and any work in a bathroom (a special location) are all notifiable." },
            { number: 7, prompt: "Under Part P, a homeowner replacing their own consumer unit without notifying anyone is committing an offence under:", options: { A: "The Consumer Protection Act 1987", B: "The Electricity Safety, Quality and Continuity Regulations 2002", C: "The Building Act 1984", D: "The Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Part P sits under the Building Act 1984. Failing to notify or comply is a Building Act offence, prosecutable up to 2 years after the work was completed. ESQCR governs DNO supplies; HSWA governs work safety; CPA governs goods sold." },
            { number: 8, prompt: "A new circuit installed for an outdoor garden lighting feature, supplied from the dwelling's consumer unit, is:", options: { A: "Non-notifiable because it is outdoors", B: "Notifiable because it is a new circuit", C: "Outside Part P because it serves an outdoor location", D: "Only notifiable if SWA cable is used" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Part P explicitly extends to land associated with the dwelling. Any new circuit is notifiable regardless of whether it runs indoors or outdoors. Kitchens and outdoor additions to existing circuits dropped from the notifiable list in 2013, but new circuits never did." },
            { number: 9, prompt: "Which item below is correctly described as a Part P \"special location\"?", options: { A: "A garage with no plumbing", B: "A kitchen with a sink", C: "A bathroom containing a bath", D: "An outdoor patio area with installed sockets" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Special locations in Part P refer principally to rooms containing a bath or shower (zones 0/1/2 of BS 7671 Section 701). Swimming pools and saunas also count under BS 7671 but are rarer. Kitchens and outdoor areas are not special locations." },
            { number: 10, prompt: "How long after completion can the local authority prosecute for failing to notify Part P work?", options: { A: "6 months", B: "12 months", C: "2 years", D: "5 years" }, answer: "C", explanation: "The Building Act 1984 sets a 2-year prosecution window from the date of the offence. The bigger long-term issue for most homeowners is the inability to evidence compliance during a sale, even after the prosecution window has closed." },
            { number: 11, prompt: "An electrician moves an existing socket-outlet 1.5 m to the right within a hallway. The circuit is unchanged. This is:", options: { A: "A new circuit and notifiable", B: "An alteration to an existing circuit, non-notifiable as it is not in a special location", C: "Outside the scope of Part P", D: "Notifiable because socket positions are protected" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Moving an accessory along an existing circuit outside a special location is an alteration — non-notifiable since 2013. A Minor Works certificate would still typically be issued." },
            { number: 12, prompt: "Replacing the main switch in an existing consumer unit (with no other changes) is treated as:", options: { A: "A non-notifiable accessory replacement", B: "Notifiable, equivalent to consumer unit replacement", C: "Notifiable only if the rating changes", D: "Outside the scope of Part P" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Replacement of the main switch is treated in the same notifiable category as replacing the consumer unit itself, because the switch is integral to fault and isolation provisions for the whole installation." },
            { number: 13, prompt: "Part P applies to the common access corridor lighting in a residential block of flats because:", options: { A: "All commercial property is automatically covered", B: "The common parts are shared parts of a building containing dwellings", C: "BS 7671 has been incorporated into Part P by reference", D: "The local authority owns the corridor" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Part P explicitly covers the common parts (corridors, stairwells, shared lobbies) of buildings containing dwellings, as well as the dwellings themselves and their associated outbuildings/gardens." },
            { number: 14, prompt: "A bathroom extractor fan is installed 250 mm from the edge of a shower at a height of 2.1 m. Is this work notifiable under Part P?", options: { A: "No, because it is above 2.25 m height", B: "Yes — it is in zones 1/2 of a special location", C: "No, because fans are accessories", D: "Only if it is a new circuit" }, answer: "B", explanation: "The position is within zone 1/2 of a room containing a shower. Any addition or alteration in a special location is notifiable, regardless of whether it is a new circuit. Kitchens lost their notifiable status in 2013; bathrooms did not." },
            { number: 15, prompt: "A non-CPS contractor wants to carry out a notifiable Part P job. Which of these is the legitimate prior step?", options: { A: "Email the customer's solicitor", B: "Submit a Building Notice or Full Plans to LABC before starting", C: "Issue an EICR before starting", D: "Notify the DNO" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Non-CPS contractors must give the local authority Building Control prior notification (Building Notice or Full Plans) and pay the inspection fee. CPS members alone can self-certify after completion." },
            { number: 16, prompt: "Under Part P, an alteration to an existing lighting circuit in a kitchen is:", options: { A: "Notifiable — kitchens are still special locations", B: "Notifiable — all kitchen work is still controlled", C: "Non-notifiable, but must comply with BS 7671", D: "Outside Part P scope" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Kitchens were removed from Part P notifiable categories in April 2013. Alterations to existing circuits in kitchens are non-notifiable but must still comply with BS 7671 and be certified appropriately (Minor Works)." },
            { number: 17, prompt: "Which of these is the correct paperwork for a notifiable Part P job involving a new shower circuit, completed by a CPS member?", options: { A: "Building Notice and EICR", B: "EIC plus a Building Regulations compliance certificate via the CPS", C: "Minor Works only", D: "No paperwork is required" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Notifiable work usually involves a new circuit or significant change, so an EIC is appropriate. The CPS member also issues (via the scheme) a Building Regulations compliance certificate within 30 days, which is sent to the homeowner and notified to the local authority." },
            { number: 18, prompt: "Replacing a single damaged 13 A socket faceplate on an unchanged ring final circuit is:", options: { A: "Notifiable as an alteration", B: "Maintenance / repair, not notifiable, not even Part P territory", C: "Notifiable only if the existing circuit is RCD-protected", D: "Notifiable because it involves making and breaking final connections" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Like-for-like replacement of an accessory is maintenance / repair. It does not trigger Part P notification because it is not an addition, alteration, new circuit, consumer unit change or special-location change." },
            { number: 19, prompt: "Part P applies to electrical installations in an outdoor pond pump in a domestic garden because:", options: { A: "Gardens count as commercial premises", B: "The pump is supplied from the dwelling and the garden is land associated with the dwelling", C: "BS 7671 specifically lists pond pumps", D: "It is mandated by ESQCR" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Part P scope explicitly extends to gardens and land associated with the dwelling. Whether a particular pond-pump job is notifiable then depends on whether it is a new circuit, alters a special location, or replaces a consumer unit." },
            { number: 20, prompt: "When a CPS-registered electrician completes notifiable Part P work, the Building Regulations compliance certificate must reach the homeowner within:", options: { A: "5 working days", B: "14 calendar days", C: "30 days", D: "90 days" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Schemes operate within a 30-day notification window: the contractor notifies the scheme, the scheme issues the compliance certificate to the homeowner and informs the local authority. Late notification can result in scheme penalties." }
