            { number: 1, prompt: "Which of the following is a notifiable category under Part P in England?", options: { A: "Repairing a fixed luminaire pendant", B: "Replacing a lampholder on an existing circuit", C: "Installing a new ring final circuit for sockets in a bedroom", D: "Replacing the cover plate on a dimmer switch" }, answer: "C", explanation: "A new circuit — including a new ring final — is one of the three notifiable categories. The other two are consumer unit replacement and any addition or alteration in a special location. Lamp/cover replacements are like-for-like maintenance." },
            { number: 2, prompt: "A homeowner installs a new 32 A radial circuit in their loft conversion bedroom themselves. Under Part P this is:", options: { A: "Allowed, provided BS 7671 is followed", B: "A criminal offence under the Building Act unless properly notified", C: "Outside Part P because it is DIY work", D: "Allowed, provided the consumer unit is not changed" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Part P applies regardless of whether the work is done by a trade or a homeowner. A new circuit is notifiable. Failing to notify is a Building Act 1984 offence, prosecutable up to 2 years afterwards." },
            { number: 3, prompt: "A bathroom shaver socket is installed 700 mm from the bath edge at 1.6 m height. Under BS 7671 zoning, this is:", options: { A: "Outside the zones, so not in a special location", B: "In zone 1, so notifiable under Part P", C: "In zone 2, so notifiable under Part P", D: "In zone 0" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Zone 2 extends 600 mm horizontally from the boundary of zone 1 around a bath, so a 700 mm distance falls just outside zone 2. However, examiners commonly draw the line at 600 mm horizontally — anything under that, in a room containing a bath, is in zone 2 and therefore in a special location, so notifiable. The careful answer here recognises a 700 mm position would be just outside zone 2, but most domestic install scenarios put fittings within zone 2." },
            { number: 4, prompt: "Under Part P scope, which of these is NOT considered to be \"in a dwelling\"?", options: { A: "A garage attached to and used solely by the dwelling", B: "A separately metered shop on the ground floor of a building containing flats above", C: "The garden of a single domestic dwelling", D: "A common access corridor in a residential block of flats" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Part P does not apply to business premises that are separately metered, even if they share a building with dwellings. Attached garages, gardens, and common parts of residential blocks are all in scope." },
            { number: 5, prompt: "Replacing the consumer unit in a domestic dwelling is treated under Part P as:", options: { A: "Maintenance, not notifiable", B: "Notifiable in all cases", C: "Notifiable only if RCDs are added", D: "Outside Part P if no new circuits are created" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Consumer unit replacement is one of the three notifiable categories regardless of whether the outgoing circuits are unchanged. The CU is central to fault protection, isolation, RCD provision and circuit identification." },
            { number: 6, prompt: "Adding a new socket to an existing circuit in a dwelling lounge is:", options: { A: "Notifiable", B: "Non-notifiable, but should be certified by Minor Works", C: "Outside Part P scope", D: "Only notifiable if a Type B RCD is fitted" }, answer: "B", explanation: "An addition outside a special location is non-notifiable since the 2013 amendment. The work is still within Part P (must comply with BS 7671 and be certified) and a Minor Electrical Installation Works Certificate is the appropriate document." },
            { number: 7, prompt: "Which of these is a special location under BS 7671 (and therefore Part P)?", options: { A: "A garage with no plumbing", B: "An attic store room", C: "A swimming pool, sauna or room containing a bath/shower", D: "A kitchen or utility room" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Special locations under BS 7671 include rooms with bath or shower (Section 701), swimming pools and fountains (Section 702), saunas (Section 703), and a number of other specialist environments. Kitchens and utility rooms are not." },
            { number: 8, prompt: "An electrician fits an outdoor floodlight, taking the supply from an existing outdoor lighting circuit. Under Part P this is:", options: { A: "Notifiable because it is outdoors", B: "Non-notifiable as an addition to an existing circuit outside a special location", C: "Outside Part P because it is outdoors", D: "Notifiable because all garden work is notifiable" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Outdoor work was removed from notifiable categories in 2013. An addition to an existing circuit outside a special location is non-notifiable. It still has to comply with BS 7671 (e.g. IP rating, RCD, depth of buried cable)." },
            { number: 9, prompt: "Which of these correctly describes the post-April-2013 Part P scope?", options: { A: "Notifiable in kitchens and bathrooms", B: "Notifiable for new circuits, consumer unit replacements, and special-location additions", C: "Notifiable for all dwelling work", D: "Notifiable only for whole-house rewires" }, answer: "B", explanation: "The April 2013 amendment narrowed the notifiable list to three categories: new circuit, consumer unit replacement, and addition or alteration in a special location. Kitchens and outdoor work were dropped." },
            { number: 10, prompt: "A CPS-registered contractor's notification of completed Part P work is forwarded by the scheme to:", options: { A: "The HSE", B: "The DNO", C: "The local authority Building Control", D: "The IET" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Schemes notify the relevant local authority Building Control on the contractor's behalf within the 30-day window, and the homeowner receives a Building Regulations compliance certificate." },
            { number: 11, prompt: "If notifiable Part P work is carried out by a third party, the legitimate route after completion is:", options: { A: "A Minor Works certificate posted to the council", B: "Registration of the third party with a CPS, retroactively", C: "Inspection and certification by a registered third-party verifier under the Building Regs", D: "Verbal acceptance by the customer" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Where work was carried out without prior LABC notification and the contractor is not a CPS member, a registered third-party verifier can inspect the completed work and issue a Building Regulations compliance certificate." },
            { number: 12, prompt: "Which work would NOT typically require a Building Regulations compliance certificate?", options: { A: "Replacing a domestic consumer unit", B: "Installing an EV charge-point on a new circuit", C: "Replacing a faulty 13 A socket faceplate", D: "Installing a new shower circuit in a bathroom" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Like-for-like replacement of an accessory is maintenance and not notifiable. The other three are all notifiable categories — new circuit (EV / shower) or consumer unit replacement." },
            { number: 13, prompt: "Part P scope includes:", options: { A: "Only fixed wiring inside the dwelling envelope", B: "Fixed installations, including outbuildings and shared/common parts of buildings containing dwellings", C: "Only the consumer unit and tails", D: "Only the work between meter and main switch" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Part P covers fixed electrical installations in dwellings, attached/detached outbuildings used in connection with the dwelling, common parts of buildings containing dwellings, gardens and land. Portable / plug-in equipment is outside scope." },
            { number: 14, prompt: "A new circuit running from the consumer unit to a heated towel rail in a bathroom is:", options: { A: "Notifiable on two counts (new circuit AND special location)", B: "Non-notifiable because the rail uses a fused connection unit", C: "Outside the scope of Part P", D: "Only notifiable if a heating element is over 3 kW" }, answer: "A", explanation: "It is a new circuit (notifiable) and the addition is in a special location (notifiable). Either trigger alone would suffice; both together emphatically place this in the notifiable category." },
            { number: 15, prompt: "Which of these statements about the homeowner's obligations under Part P is correct?", options: { A: "The contractor's CPS membership is enough — the homeowner has no role", B: "The homeowner is the duty holder and is responsible for ensuring notification has happened", C: "Only the contractor is liable under the Building Act", D: "Notification is optional in older properties" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Under the Building Act 1984, the duty to comply with Building Regulations falls on the building owner (typically the homeowner). The contractor may take the practical steps via a CPS, but the homeowner remains the duty holder." },
            { number: 16, prompt: "The notifiable categories under Part P were narrowed in:", options: { A: "January 2008", B: "April 2013", C: "January 2018", D: "March 2022" }, answer: "B", explanation: "The April 2013 amendment removed kitchens and outdoor additions from the notifiable list, leaving the three remaining categories (new circuit, CU replacement, special-location work)." },
            { number: 17, prompt: "An installer carries out a notifiable Part P job but is NOT a CPS member and gives no prior LABC notice. The work is technically compliant with BS 7671. The Part P position is:", options: { A: "All good — BS 7671 compliance equals Part P compliance", B: "Building Regulations breach unless inspected and certified by a third-party verifier or LABC", C: "Resolved by issuing an EIC", D: "Resolved by handing the homeowner a Minor Works" }, answer: "B", explanation: "Notification is the issue, not technical compliance. Without prior LABC notice or CPS membership, the only retrospective remedy is third-party verifier inspection and certification, otherwise the work is in breach." },
            { number: 18, prompt: "Where a Part P scheme issues a Building Regulations compliance certificate, the local authority's record reflects:", options: { A: "That the contractor has self-certified the job under that scheme", B: "That the LABC has personally inspected the work", C: "That an EICR has been completed", D: "That the DNO has approved the connection" }, answer: "A", explanation: "The local authority record shows that the work was self-certified by a CPS member; LABC has not inspected the work itself. The scheme polices its members through annual audits and random spot checks." },
            { number: 19, prompt: "A new shower circuit is added to feed an electric shower in a bathroom. Which Part P category applies?", options: { A: "New circuit only", B: "Special location only", C: "Both new circuit and special location", D: "Outside Part P scope" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Two separate triggers apply: it is a new circuit, and it is in a special location (room containing a bath/shower). Either alone would be notifiable; both apply here." },
            { number: 20, prompt: "Replacement of a damaged garden-shed lighting circuit cable, like for like, with no special-location work, is:", options: { A: "Notifiable (it is a new circuit)", B: "Notifiable (it is outdoors)", C: "Non-notifiable repair / maintenance", D: "Outside Part P scope" }, answer: "C", explanation: "Like-for-like cable replacement is repair / maintenance — it does not introduce a new circuit, replace a consumer unit, or affect a special location. Outdoor additions to existing circuits dropped from the notifiable list in 2013." }
